  "worked_solution": "First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/8490920052778795ca03072fd0a6933b4abc5756f2e75ae9f61863d3726dd5c8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $6.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,6),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/b5deca2c07bc8c8a5e58c28a6ca2449268999a54b21407380ae168aafabd7a5d.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,6)$ to $(0,10),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220062/assets/57ac1f2daa7ee44b5cf9fbd2d4c9ce8a25edc3a3053af4d9164b7f8e83b9e00f.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
